  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light southerly flow will steadily increase tonight \ninto Thursday in advance of an approaching cold front. This combined \nwith warm low-level thicknesses will support one more day of above \nnormal temperatures. Record highs are not expected, however.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A potent surface low is forecast to translate east \nfrom the Ohio Valley to the Northern Mid-Atlantic Thursday and \nFriday. Meanwhile, an associated strong cold front is forecast to \ncross ENC Thursday night. A secondary cold front is then forecast to \nmove through late Friday. Moderate to strong CAA behind each front \nwill lead to a fairly drastic drop in temperatures compared to the \nabove normal temperatures will are currently experiencing. Guidance \nsuggests multiple days of highs in the 50s, with lows in the 30s and \n40s. Normal highs this time of year are in the mid to upper 60s, and \nnormal lows are in the 40s and 50s. The incoming airmass will be \nrunning a solid 10+ degrees below those normal values. Probabilistic \nguidance shows the highest risk of sub-freezing temperatures coming \nSaturday night into Sunday morning, although this risk could extend \nto other nights as well. Closer to the coast, some areas away from \nmarine influences could see frost conditions if/where winds decouple \nenough. Assuming guidance doesn't change, frost/freeze headlines \nwill likely be needed for a large portion of the area this weekend \nand maybe into early next week.\n\nIn addition to the cold, each front will be accompanied by a period \nof gusty winds of 20-30 mph. This will add to the chill, with wind \nchill values inland making it feel more like the 20s each night from \nFriday through Sunday. The winds may lead to elevated soundside\nwaters, but winds do not currently look to be strong enough to\ncause significant impacts. Perhaps the greatest risk will be\nareas around Roanoke Island that can see some impacts in\nwesterly flow. This risk would be focused in the Thursday night\nthrough Friday period.",
